#An agent control plane for BSS, OSS, and the network

Cognition proposes. Control owns the session. Execution authorizes. Action mutates. Knowledge says what is true. Evidence says what was done. Models and graphs live only in the planner.

This is not an agent architecture. It is a **carrier-grade reference architecture that governs how agents are allowed to operate against BSS, OSS, and live network systems.** That distinction matters the moment a probabilistic planner can influence a customer record, service order, network resource, spectrum decision, eSIM activation, or configuration change.

The requirement is larger than orchestration. The reference architecture has to define the common contracts every engineering pod builds against: how agents reason, how multi-turn and multi-agent work is owned, how humans and agents hand work off, how identities and resources are grounded, how read and write authority are separated, how Systems of Record are mutated, how actions are made idempotent and auditable, and what evidence is required before an agent may move from assist to bounded autonomous execution.

The runtime architecture is expressed as **L1-L5**. A separate **L6 Assurance & Evaluation layer** certifies those runtime layers and governs the assist-to-automate continuum. L6 is not another hop in a production transaction; it is the evidence system that determines whether the behavior of L1-L5 is safe and reliable enough to promote.

The architecture spans **Platform & Systems, AI & Agentic, and Data & Ontology** concerns, but those tracks are not separate products. They converge in one reference blueprint, one action model, one set of NFRs, and one assurance framework.

The central rule remains:

**Cognition may propose. Agent Control may own. Execution may authorize. Action alone may mutate. Knowledge may inform. Evidence must prove.**

Most agent drawings collapse planning, tool calls, and side effects into one box. Fine for a desktop copilot. Not fine when a retry can shut a port, duplicate a work order, or race a technician on the same circuit. The correct split is older than LLMs: think, own the work, authorize the change, act — then look at what actually happened. The model is only the first of those steps.

