## L1-L6 reference blueprint

L1-L5 are the runtime reference architecture instantiated by engineering pods. L6 is the assurance layer that evaluates and certifies the behavior of those runtime layers.

| Level | Reference layer | Owns | Typical components | Must not do | Concrete failure if violated — Telco / Industrial |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **L1** | Experience & external contracts | Northbound interaction, caller identity, session entry, external API contract | UI, NOC, CSR, external APIs, peer agents, REST / GraphQL / gRPC / A2A | Hide machine ambiguity inside a conversational contract | **Telco:** NOC UI says “traffic rerouted successfully” when only the request was accepted; no network evidence exists. **Industrial:** HMI says a machine is isolated when the PLC/interlock state never changed. |
| **L2** | Agent & cognition | Interpretation, retrieval, planning, recommendation, specialist-agent reasoning | LLMs, LangGraph, specialist graphs, read-only tools, grounding calls | Hold write credentials or commit to a SoR | **Telco:** a model turns an alarm directly into a vendor-specific RAN command or invents a cell/port identifier. **Industrial:** a maintenance agent hallucinates a PLC tag or setpoint and treats it as executable identity. |
| **L3** | Control & governance | Session ownership, handoff, locks, authority, risk, policy, approval, idempotency, ledger | Agent Control, Execution Control, policy engine, reservation/lock service, operation ledger | Allow a better plan to bypass an existing owner, policy, approval, or execution record | **Telco:** a RAN energy optimizer sleeps a carrier while a separate remediation workflow is steering traffic onto it because neither owns/reserves the resource canonically. **Industrial:** two agents issue conflicting production-line mode changes because a maintenance lock or active owner was bypassed. |
| **L4** | Action & integration | Declared-action registry, typed mutation, callable binding, protocol mapping, commit/abort, compensation, southbound execution | Action registry, typed adapters, TMF, YANG, NETCONF/gNMI, REST/gRPC/MQ/vendor APIs | Let free-form model output or an unregistered verb become a southbound write | **Telco:** `STEER_TRAFFIC` resolves to the wrong vendor parameter or a legacy CLI sequence partially applies and is falsely reported as rolled back. **Industrial:** `CHANGE_SETPOINT` binds to the wrong PLC register or leaves a partially applied machine configuration. |
| **L5** | Systems, knowledge & operational truth | Systems of Record, governed Source-of-Truth projection, ontology, telemetry, evidence | BSS, OSS, network controllers/devices, inventory, topology, ontology, SoT projection, Evidence | Allow model/session/vector/planning state to become a hidden SoR | **Telco:** stale topology/telemetry says a neighboring cell has spare capacity, so traffic is steered into an already saturated cell. **Industrial:** a stale digital-twin projection says a machine is idle while the physical line is still running. |
| **L6** | Assurance & evaluation | Evidence that L1-L5 satisfy deterministic, behavioral, semantic, trajectory, safety, and production reliability requirements | Hard-invariant tests, Conjecture, LLM Factory, TauBench-style environments, Collinear-style simulation, production regression harvesting | Become part of execution authority or substitute an LLM judge for a deterministic control | **Telco:** a new model performs well on happy-path RAN tests but a stale-state or failed-wake regression reaches production because it was never ratcheted into the suite. **Industrial:** a model/runtime upgrade changes action selection and a safety-critical trajectory regresses without a deterministic or adversarial test catching it. |

The paired failure examples are intentionally domain-specific: the same architectural seam fails differently in Telco and industrial systems, while the underlying control law remains portable.

The L6 layer contains several distinct evaluation regimes:

1. **Hard/system invariants** — exact deterministic assertions over schemas, identity, permissions, target binding, state transitions, idempotency, risk policy, rollback, and action constraints.
2. **Behavioral invariants/contracts** — repeated scenario execution against MUST / MUST NOT / MAY commitments; measured as empirical behavioral reliability, not model self-confidence.
3. **Cognition goldens** — paired input/ground-truth datasets for intent, extraction, classification, grounding candidates, recommendations, and structured plans.
4. **Agent trajectories** — environment fixtures with tools, permissions, state, and valid terminal outcomes; multiple reasoning paths may be acceptable.
5. **Adversarial/failure simulation** — stale state, conflicts, retries, partial writes, tool failures, malicious or ambiguous inputs, and recovery behavior.
6. **Production evidence** — traces, ledger, incidents, actual-vs-intended state, drift, and failure harvesting back into the regression corpus.

Every pod must conform to the L1-L6 authority model, but **every pod does not implement all six layers**.

Each pod declares a **conformance envelope**:

- **IMPLEMENTS** — the L1-L6 responsibilities owned by this pod;
- **CONSUMES** — contracts or authoritative outputs owned elsewhere;
- **MUST NOT OWN** — authorities that would create a shadow control plane or shadow SoR;
- **N/A** — requirements genuinely outside the pod's scope;
- **EVIDENCE** — the L6 tests and telemetry proving the applicable contracts hold.

The rule is:

> **Every pod conforms to L1-L6; each pod implements only the layers and responsibilities in its declared conformance envelope.**

L1-L6 is therefore not a six-layer implementation checklist. It is a six-layer **authority model**. A frontend pod can be primarily L1. A workflow pod can be L2/L3. A telemetry pod can be L5/L6. What matters is that none silently takes authority assigned to another layer.


## Why agentic systems elevate the “Must Not Do” risks

Most of the failure modes in this architecture existed before LLMs. Agentic systems do not invent distributed-systems risk; they **remove many of the implicit constraints that previously kept those risks bounded**.

That changes the engineering problem in several ways:

- **Behavior is no longer fully enumerated in code.** Traditional systems mostly executed paths engineers explicitly authored. Agents infer intent, select actions, and compose sequences at runtime, so the reachable behavior surface is larger than the static call graph.

- **Semantic interpretation now sits directly upstream of execution.** A conventional API caller usually knows which operation it is invoking. An agent first interprets language, telemetry, another agent's output, or retrieved context. A semantic mistake can become an operational mistake unless grounding and execution authority are independent seams.

- **Schema-compliant tool calling can create false confidence.** A valid JSON/tool schema proves shape, not authority. It does not prove that the target is correct, the workflow state is valid, the resource is unowned, the action is safe, or the operation should execute now.

- **Safety logic can become fragmented across tools.** Teams can put locks, RBAC, idempotency, approvals, and blast-radius checks inside each tool function. At scale, that creates hundreds of miniature control planes with inconsistent policy and audit behavior.

- **Agents can create authority by implication.** Statements such as “traffic has been rerouted” or “I opened the incident” can cause a UI, another agent, or an operator to treat proposed work as completed work. Evidence must own execution truth.

- **Multi-agent systems introduce new contention.** Several autonomous agents may independently decide to optimize, repair, provision, or reconfigure the same resource. Without canonical ownership and reservation, a locally better plan can steal authority from an in-flight one.

- **Local optimization can become global failure.** One RAN optimizer sleeping a carrier may be reasonable. Hundreds doing the same thing concurrently can create a network-wide capacity problem. Autonomy increases the need for global blast-radius, capacity, reservation, and policy controls.

- **The system can act at machine speed.** A weak control seam that once produced one bad change can now produce a cascade before a human notices.

- **Retries are semantically dangerous.** An agent may reinterpret a timeout, alter its plan, call a different tool, or delegate to another agent. Without a stable logical operation identity, retries can become new intentions rather than retries of the same transaction.

- **Long-running conversational state can drift from authoritative state.** Context, summaries, memories, active tasks, and handoffs may outlive the facts they were based on. Conversation state must remain a projection, not a hidden SoR.

- **Handoffs can look successful while losing the work.** Changing the active agent is not the same thing as transferring typed work, grounded targets, ownership, and an executable continuation. This is the Hollow Act failure.

- **Models and prompts can change behavior without changing interfaces.** A model, prompt, retrieval corpus, context-composition rule, or tool description can materially alter behavior while every API schema stays the same. Semantic and behavioral drift therefore become first-class production risks.

- **Agent plans cross more trust boundaries.** A single reasoning loop can move from customer language to ontology, inventory, workflow, billing, OSS, and network mutation. Each step changes the kind of authority involved.

- **Failures are less reproducible.** Probabilistic behavior can disappear on rerun. Traditional debugging is therefore insufficient; repeated behavioral fixtures, trajectory tests, adversarial simulation, and production evidence become necessary.

- **The model can invent completeness.** An LLM can fluently manufacture an identifier, state, status, or completion claim. Identity, arithmetic, ownership, state transitions, and execution evidence must therefore remain code-owned.

The distinction can be summarized as:

> **Traditional software primarily asks: “Is this code path correct?” Agentic software must additionally ask: “Who interpreted the intent, who owns the work, what is grounded, what may happen next, who is authorized to mutate state, and what proves it actually happened?”**

That is why the **Must Not Do** column matters. It identifies where agent autonomy would otherwise blur the boundaries between meaning, ownership, authority, execution, and evidence.

> **The purpose of the control plane is not to eliminate agent autonomy. It is to restore explicit constraints at the seams where probabilistic reasoning crosses into operational authority.**


