## Source of Truth versus System of Record

These labels get used as synonyms. They are not. Mixing them is how an agent cache becomes a second inventory.

**System of Record (SoR)** is the operational system that **owns writes** for a class of entity. Authority, mutation audit, and reconciliation start there.

- BSS / CRM is typically SoR for customer, product, commercial order.
- Service inventory / orchestration is typically SoR for logical service and in-flight order state.
- Resource inventory is often SoR for designed resources — and is frequently wrong about the live device.
- The device, controller, or EMS is SoR for running configuration and operational state.
- ITSM is SoR for the ticket. Change management is SoR for the change record.

An SoR is allowed to disagree with every other system. That disagreement is why the rest of the architecture exists.

**Source of Truth (SoT)** is the projection the agent is **allowed to plan against**. It is not a second owner of writes. It is a decision surface: ontology-typed, instance-resolved, explicit about freshness and provenance.

Sometimes one SoR is so trusted for one attribute that it is also the SoT for that attribute — ifOperStatus comes from the device, not from last week’s discovery job. Sometimes the SoT is a composed graph: customer from BSS, designed path from inventory, live status from telemetry, lock from Agent Control. Composition does not transfer write ownership.

| QuestionAnswer                           |                                                                        |
| ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Who may mutate this object?              | The SoR, via Action, after Execution says yes.                         |
| What may Cognition believe right now?    | The SoT projection, with timestamps and provenance.                    |
| What if SoT and SoR disagree?            | Drift. Observe. Do not write the cache back as if it were the network. |
| May the model or LangGraph state be SoT? | No. Session memory is a scratchpad.                                    |
| May the Knowledge plane become an SoR?   | No. That is a shadow OSS.                                              |

In this architecture the Knowledge plane is SoT. Southbound systems are SoRs. Grounding is how a sentence becomes an SoR identifier. Execution is how a plan is allowed to change an SoR. Observe is how the SoT is allowed to move after the SoR moves.

Test: if the agent plane disappeared tonight, which systems would still be allowed to run the business? Those are the SoRs. If the answer includes “the vector store” or “the graph the model has been writing to,” the design has already drifted.

Planning against a raw SoR is often impossible — the device does not know the customer, the CRM does not know the interface, none of them speak ontology. Planning only against a cache is how you configure a port that was renamed yesterday. SoT without SoR is a museum. SoR without SoT is a pile of protocols. The closed loop is the contract between them.

