## Agent runtimes, orchestration, and the control plane

Agent runtime, multi-agent orchestration, and carrier-grade control are related but different responsibilities.

| Concern | Owns | Possible implementations | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Agent runtime** | Model calls, local memory/state, tool loops, retries, interrupts | LangGraph, OpenAI Agents SDK, Google ADK, custom/Bot0 runtime | May reason and propose; no implicit SoR authority |
| **Multi-agent orchestration** | Delegation, specialist routing, handoffs, subagents, conversational/task progression | LangGraph, OpenAI Agents SDK, Google ADK, Bot0 Agent Control, custom orchestrator | Coordinates reasoning and work; handoff does not automatically transfer canonical object ownership |
| **Agent Control** | Canonical session owner, object ownership/reservations, contention, durable handoff, human takeover | Bot0 control plane or dedicated platform services; durable workflow substrate may help | Owns who has the work, not whether a mutation is allowed |
| **Execution Control** | Authority, risk class, policy, approval, operation identity, idempotency, ledger, pause/escalate, rollback/compensation | Bot0 control plane plus policy/workflow/ledger services | Decides whether a proposed mutation may happen |
| **Action** | Typed southbound mutation and protocol truth | Adapters, TMF/YANG/vendor clients, network APIs | Only layer that mutates a SoR |

The useful distinction is:

**The orchestrator coordinates reasoning. The control plane coordinates authoritative ownership and execution rights.**

Sometimes one implementation may host both. A Bot0-native agent may use Bot0 for orchestration and control. A LangGraph, OpenAI, ADK, or other agent may use its own runtime and still participate in the same carrier control plane. The reference architecture therefore does not mandate an agent framework.

```text
LangGraph agent ─────┐
OpenAI agent ────────┤
ADK agent ───────────┼──> Agent Control
Bot0-native agent ───┤        │
Other runtime ───────┘        ▼
                         Execution Control
                                │
                              Action
```

A framework-level handoff such as “delegate to the Network Remediation Agent” is not equivalent to transferring authoritative ownership of `CELL-417`. The first is orchestration. The second is a control-plane transaction with identity, ownership, policy, evidence, and potentially a lock or reservation.

