## Multi-agent and human-agent workflows

Carrier workflows are not single-agent chats. A single outcome may involve a customer-service agent, assurance agent, resource/inventory agent, network-change agent, provisioning agent, and a human operator. The architecture represents **ownership, authority, and evidence** across those actors rather than relying on agents to coordinate by free-text consensus.

| Workflow | Participants | L2: cognition / specialists | L3: ownership & execution control | L4: typed action | L5: truth & evidence | Human participation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Network update / configuration change** | NOC operator, assurance agent, network-change agent | Diagnose condition, draft typed change, estimate impact | Lock resources, classify risk, verify window/authority, approval, idempotency, blast-radius policy | NETCONF/gNMI/vendor API commit, abort or compensation | Running config, topology, telemetry, change record, ledger, post-change observation | Approve high-risk change; take ownership on escalation |
| **Request / allocate network resource** | Capacity agent, inventory agent, planning agent, engineer | Determine demand and candidate resource/capacity fit | Reserve resource, prevent concurrent allocation, policy/authority checks | Create/update reservation, service/resource order or inventory object | Inventory, topology, service-resource graph, reservation state, ledger | Resolve scarcity/conflict or non-standard allocation |
| **Spectrum anomaly / interference** | Spectrum-analysis agent, assurance agent, NOC | Correlate measurements, classify anomaly, propose investigation/remediation | Own affected cell/domain, block conflicting remediation, determine autonomy/risk | Trigger measurements, bounded parameter changes, create incident/change | Spectrum measurements, RF inventory, alarms, topology, incident/change SoRs | Resolve ambiguous root cause; authorize disruptive remediation |
| **eSIM registration / activation** | Digital/CSR channel, identity/KYC service, provisioning agent, BSS/OSS agent | Validate request/eligibility and construct activation plan; propose declared action `ACTIVATE_ESIM` against grounded Subscriber/Service instances | Bind subscriber/device/service identity, enforce fraud/policy, dedupe request, validate that `ACTIVATE_ESIM` is registered and admissible | Resolve `ACTIVATE_ESIM` to the correct BSS/provisioning callable; create/activate subscription/profile and update service/order state | CRM/subscriber, entitlement, order/provisioning state, SM-DP+/related systems, ledger | Handle identity/fraud exceptions and failed activation |
| **Order fallout / activation recovery** | Order agent, provisioning agent, inventory agent, operations | Diagnose failed step across order/service/resource state | Establish one owner, specialist handoff, retry/compensation policy | Retry bounded step, create work order, update order/service state | Commercial/service order, inventory, telemetry, ticket/change record | Perform physical/manual work or approve exception path |
| **Billing/service dispute** | CSR, billing agent, entitlement agent, network-evidence agent | Inspect plan, usage, entitlement and service evidence; recommend disposition | Own case, enforce credit authority/escalation thresholds | Apply approved credit/adjustment or create case | CRM/billing, usage, entitlement, policy version, ledger | Approve high-value credit or resolve policy ambiguity |

Multi-agent coordination belongs in **L3 Agent Control**, not in prompt convention. A specialist agent acquires or receives ownership through a typed handoff; object reservations prevent conflicting work; Execution Control independently decides whether a proposed mutation may happen.

Partly human workflows use the same protocol. A human can enter at L1, receive ownership in L3, approve or refuse an execution decision, perform a manual task represented in workflow state, and leave evidence in L5. Human takeover is therefore a controlled session/object transfer, not a chat interruption.

A typical multi-agent flow is:

```text
detect / request
      ↓
specialist cognition (L2)
      ↓
ground to ontology + live instances (L5 knowledge)
      ↓
acquire / transfer ownership (L3 Agent Control)
      ↓
risk + authority + idempotency + approval (L3 Execution Control)
      ↓
typed mutation (L4 Action)
      ↓
observe actual SoR state (L5)
      ↓
close evidence / release or hand off ownership (L3/L5)
      ↓
L6 captures the run as evaluation / production evidence
```

