| **Durable workflow / execution** | **Temporal**, Camunda, AWS Step Functions, Azure Durable Functions, Conductor/Orkes, Restate | Durable state, retries, timers, signals, long-running workflows, human waits, recovery; Temporal is particularly strong for durable execution and saga/compensation | Ontology grounding, canonical agent/object ownership semantics, Telco risk policy, SoR authority, autonomy certification |
| **Agent runtime / multi-agent orchestration** | **LangGraph / LangSmith Deployment**, OpenAI Agents SDK, Google ADK, Semantic Kernel, custom runtimes | Agent loops, local state, handoffs, subagents, tool calls, memory, human-in-the-loop; some provide durable execution/deployment | Cross-runtime canonical ownership, SoR-safe write governance, enterprise-wide action authority |
| **Policy / authorization** | OPA, Cedar, Cerbos, custom policy engine | Deterministic allow/deny decisions over identity, resource, action and context | Agent/session ownership, durable workflow, ontology resolution, mutation execution |
| **API / action gateway** | Kong, Apigee, Envoy, custom typed action services | AuthN/Z integration, rate limits, routing, API policy, typed service exposure | Semantic grounding, operation lifecycle, agent risk class, business compensation |
| **Durable state / ledger / audit** | PostgreSQL/event store, Temporal histories, append-only ledger services, observability stacks | Operation history, workflow state, replay, traces | Does not by itself define whether an agent was authorized to act or whether the target was semantically correct |
| **Agent eval / simulation** | Conjecture, LLM Factory, TauBench-style harnesses, Collinear AI, LangSmith, Braintrust | Behavioral reliability, goldens, trajectory simulation, traces, regression | Runtime enforcement; evaluation evidence must feed policy but must not become the authority boundary |
| **Ontology / knowledge / governed data** | Knowledge graphs, semantic layers, TMF SID / 3GPP NRM / YANG mappings, data-governance platforms | Canonical types, relationships, lineage, provenance, policy/data semantics | Session ownership, durable execution, southbound mutation |

### Where Temporal fits

Temporal is especially interesting because several control-plane concerns are **durable workflow concerns**:

- long-running agent or human-agent work;
- timers and change windows;
- retries after infrastructure failure;
- signals and human approval;
- durable state across process crashes;
- compensation / saga patterns;
- waiting for external state to converge;
- durable task progression.

Temporal can therefore be a strong implementation substrate **under Agent Control and Execution Control**.

But Temporal does not eliminate the need to define the carrier-specific control semantics. The workflow still needs application-level answers to:

- Who canonically owns this session, incident, order, circuit, or cell?
- What constitutes a valid typed handoff?
- Which ontology type and live instance does the proposed action target?
- Which SoR owns the write?
- Which verbs is this agent allowed to execute on this object class?
- What risk class and blast-radius limit apply?
- What constitutes an idempotent logical operation?
- Which evidence is required before autonomy is promoted?
- What is the authoritative intended-versus-observed success condition?

Temporal can durably execute those decisions; **the reference architecture defines what those decisions mean**.

The same is true of LangGraph and other agent runtimes. LangGraph can orchestrate stateful single- or multi-agent reasoning and human handoffs. Temporal can durably coordinate long-running distributed work. An enterprise may use both. Neither should be treated as the source of carrier execution authority simply because it owns runtime state.

A reasonable implementation spectrum is:

```text
OPTION A — Bot0 owns orchestration + control
Agent runtime → Bot0 Agent Control → Bot0 Execution Control → Action

OPTION B — external agent runtime, Bot0 governs
LangGraph / OpenAI / ADK → Bot0 Agent Control → Execution Control → Action

OPTION C — durable workflow substrate underneath control
Agent runtime → Agent Control / Execution semantics
                         │
                         ▼
                      Temporal
                durable workflow/state
                         │
                         ▼
                       Action

OPTION D — hybrid
LangGraph / OpenAI / ADK for agent reasoning
Temporal for durable workflow execution
Bot0 / platform services for ownership, authority, ledger, ontology-grounded action policy
```

This makes the platform **runtime-neutral** while keeping the high-value control semantics proprietary and consistent across engineering pods.

