## Control-plane options and architectural differentiation

The primitives in this reference architecture are not individually novel. Durable workflows, policy engines, agent runtimes, knowledge graphs, typed APIs, event logs, and evaluation platforms all exist. The differentiation is in **where authority is placed and how the pieces are composed into one control contract**.

| Control-plane idea | Existing analogue | What this architecture adds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Pluggable agent runtime / orchestration** | LangGraph, OpenAI Agents SDK, Google ADK, custom runtimes | Agent reasoning and handoff are explicitly separated from authoritative ownership and write rights |
| **Canonical session and object ownership** | Agent supervisors, workflow state, locks/reservations | Ownership is a first-class control transaction; a better plan does not silently steal an in-flight circuit, order, incident, or subscriber |
| **Durable workflow substrate** | Temporal, Camunda, Step Functions, Conductor/Orkes, Restate | Durability and retries are implementation substrate; carrier-specific authority, ontology, risk, SoR, and declared-action semantics remain explicit platform contracts |
| **Execution Control as a kernel** | Policy engines, workflow engines, API gateways | Authority + risk + idempotency + approval + re-grounding + operation identity + ledger + blast-radius + recovery are one choke point |
| **Declared semantic actions** | Function calling, typed APIs, command patterns | Free text becomes a closed semantic enum, grounded to an ontology type/instance, adjudicated, then bound to a registered callable |
| **Ontology → action vocabulary → callable** | Knowledge graphs, schemas, service catalogs | Separates meaning, authority, and implementation: the agent speaks `ACTIVATE_ESIM` or `SHUT_PORT`, not a vendor method |
| **Semantic authority ≠ execution authority** | Least privilege, approval workflows | Made explicit across turns, multi-agent collaboration, human takeover, and SoR writes |
| **SoT for planning; SoR for mutation** | Traditional SoT/SoR data architecture | The agent may plan against a governed projection without turning that projection, graph, vector store, or session state into a shadow OSS |
| **Evidence over conversational claims** | Audit logs, event sourcing, workflow histories | “Done” is owned by observed execution evidence and ledger state, not by the model's previous prose |
| **L6 assurance separate from runtime** | Eval platforms, CI, safety testing | Hard invariants, behavioral reliability, cognition goldens, trajectories, adversarial simulation, and production evidence are treated as distinct assurance regimes |
| **Probabilistic-to-deterministic ratchet** | Shift-left testing, formal-methods intuition | When a behavioral failure can be made impossible in code, it moves into a hard invariant so the platform stops paying inference to rediscover it |

The defensible claim is therefore not that no vendor has these ingredients. It is that the reference architecture makes a specific synthesis:

```text
free text
  → ontology-shaped meaning
  → declared semantic action
  → grounded live instance
  → canonical owner
  → execution adjudication
  → registered callable
  → SoR mutation
  → observed evidence
```

and combines it with:

```text
behavioral failure
  → measured regression
  → stronger contract
  → deterministic refusal where possible
```


### Open-source Control Plane SDK — source of the portable law

The portable authority and continuum rules in this reference architecture come from the open-source **Conversation Control Plane SDK**, not from a Telco-specific workflow engine.

Repository:

https://github.com/walidnegm/conversation-control-plane

SDK specification:

https://github.com/walidnegm/conversation-control-plane/blob/main/docs/conversation-control-plane-sdk.md

The SDK makes the ownership split explicit:

```text
SDK owns:
  who owns the turn
  what transitions are admissible
  durable product-task authority
  lifecycle / claim / ledger law

Host application owns:
  operation enum values
  kinds / phases
  continuum edges
  domain workflow topology
  product surfaces
  execution runtime
```

Its own wording is deliberately narrow:

> **SDK = who owns the turn + what transitions are admissible + durable ownership SoR. App = what ops / kinds / surfaces / edges mean + how the product speaks.**

The SDK also states that it **does not hardcode any product continuum table**, and that the specialist or host **owns the product phase machine**. That distinction is important for Telco: RF optimization, eSIM activation, order fallout, and billing adjustment workflows can each have independent graphs without forcing the central control plane to know every domain transition.

The SDK therefore supplies the **portable law**, while the domain graph supplies the **topology**.

### Competitive landscape — where the continuum contract is different

The market contains increasingly capable agent orchestration and workflow primitives. The distinction should be stated narrowly and defensibly.

| Product / pattern | What it natively gives you | What is still application / control-plane policy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **OpenAI Agents SDK** | Agent loops, tools, structured function inputs, sessions, handoffs, agents-as-tools, guardrails, human-in-the-loop, tracing | Canonical object ownership across runtimes, a carrier-specific task continuum, `TERMINATES` vs `CONTINUES` as a platform contract, a required operational next act, take-once work claims, ontology-to-SoR action semantics |
| **LangGraph / LangChain handoffs** | Persistent state, active-agent / step state, graph routing, `Command` transitions, subgraphs, tool-based handoffs, multi-turn state | Whether the transferred state constitutes authoritative work ownership; whether a semantic act became a complete typed operational act; carrier authority, SoR write policy, blast radius, ledger semantics |
| **Google ADK / similar multi-agent runtimes** | Coordinator/subagent patterns and model-driven agent transfer | Cross-runtime canonical ownership, operation registry, delivery-mode contract, next-act completeness, SoR execution authority |
| **Temporal** | Durable workflows, event history, timers, retries, signals, deterministic replay, long-running state and external Activities | Semantic action meaning, ontology grounding, who owns a carrier object, whether a handoff is operationally complete, declared-action policy, SoR-safe execution rights |
| **Generic function / tool calling** | Typed or schema-validated invocation arguments | Whether the function represents a registered semantic operation, whether it is legal on the grounded ontology target, whether continuation/ownership/next-act obligations are satisfied |
| **Bot0 / Conversation Control Plane contract** | Closed semantic acts + durable task authority + lifecycle/continuum law + delivery mode + typed handoff + owner + resolved refs + take-once claim + authoritative execution/evidence boundaries | Domain workflow topology / next-act edges, ontology, policies, adapters and deployment choices supplied by the host platform |

The differentiation is therefore **not “we invented handoffs”** or “we invented workflows.” Those are established ideas.

The more specific contribution is the contract that prevents a handoff from being counted as progress unless the work has become operational, **without requiring the Control Plane to own the domain graph**:

```text
MEANING
closed operation enum
      ↓
CONTINUUM
new / continue / clarify / resume / complete
      ↓
DELIVERY
terminates / continues
      ↓
OPERATIONALIZATION
owner + typed slots + grounded refs
      ↓
NEXT ACT
declared by domain graph
+ validated against registry
      ↓
CLAIM
take-once
      ↓
EXECUTION / EVIDENCE
```

That is a stronger invariant than “the active agent changed.”

Current vendor documentation reinforces the boundary:

- OpenAI Agents SDK handoffs transfer to a specialist and make that specialist active for the remainder of the current turn; handoffs are presented as function-like tools. Its guardrail documentation also distinguishes tool guardrails from handoff behavior.
- LangChain/LangGraph handoffs commonly update persistent state such as `active_agent` / `current_step` and route to another node or agent.
- Temporal durably records workflow commands/events and replays workflow state, while external effects live in Activities.

All are useful substrates. None of those primitives, by themselves, defines the full carrier-specific semantic-to-operational completeness contract above.

