## Specific OSS/BSS declared-action flow

Example: a customer reports that a new eSIM was purchased but never activated. The workflow spans BSS identity/order state and OSS/provisioning state.

User request:

> “My new eSIM is still not active. Fix it.”

The platform does not turn that sentence into an arbitrary tool call.

```text
L1 — NORTHBOUND
CSR / digital channel
caller = csr-1182
session = sess-77
       │
       ▼
L2 — COGNITION / ORCHESTRATION
resolve intent:
  activation_failure

ground:
  Subscriber: SUB-88319
  ProductOrder: PO-41127
  Service: MOBILE-99172

inspect governed SoT projection:
  commercial order = COMPLETE
    ← BSS order-management SoR
  entitlement      = VALID
    ← BSS product / entitlement SoR
  profile state    = DOWNLOADED
    ← eSIM provisioning / SM-DP+ evidence
  service state    = PENDING_ACTIVATION
    ← OSS / service-orchestration SoR

  each fact carries:
    canonical instance id
    source / provenance
    observed or effective timestamp
    freshness / TTL
    schema / ontology version

proposed declared action:
  ACTIVATE_ESIM
       │
       ▼
L3 — AGENT CONTROL
case owner = provisioning-agent
reserve Subscriber / Service
no competing activation workflow

EXECUTION CONTROL
is ACTIVATE_ESIM registered?        YES
target type valid?                   Subscriber / Service
caller / agent authorized?           YES
duplicate operation?                 NO
fraud / KYC policy satisfied?        YES
risk class                            MEDIUM
human approval required?             NO
operation id                          OP-ESIM-7712
       │
       ▼
L4 — ACTION REGISTRY
ACTIVATE_ESIM
  target = MOBILE-99172
  SoR = mobile provisioning platform
  binding = ProvisioningAdapter.activate_esim()

adapter performs:
  create / reconcile activation request
  update service-order state
  invoke provisioning / SM-DP+ integration
       │
       ▼
L5 — SoRs / KNOWLEDGE / EVIDENCE
BSS:
  ProductOrder PO-41127

OSS / provisioning:
  Service MOBILE-99172
  eSIM profile / activation state

OBSERVE:
  service state = ACTIVE
  profile state = ENABLED

ledger:
  declared_action = ACTIVATE_ESIM
  operation_id    = OP-ESIM-7712
  target          = MOBILE-99172
  callable        = ProvisioningAdapter.activate_esim
  result          = SUCCESS
```

The important boundary is that **`ACTIVATE_ESIM` remains the semantic action all the way through control and audit**. The BSS/OSS implementation may change by vendor, product, or market without changing what Cognition is allowed to propose.

Here, **“inspect governed SoT projection” does not mean Bot0 owns or copies those operational facts.** It means Cognition receives a read-only, ontology-typed decision surface assembled from the systems that actually own each fact. The commercial-order fact may come from BSS order management; the entitlement from product/charging or subscriber systems; the eSIM profile state from the provisioning domain; and the service state from OSS/service orchestration. The projection normalizes those facts into one decision model while preserving source, identity, freshness, and provenance.

The point is to prevent a model from reasoning over four unrelated API payloads or, worse, over stale conversational memory. If one source is stale or cannot be resolved, the SoT projection says so; it does not silently manufacture a clean state.


A second OSS example works the same way:

```text
utterance:
  "Shut the noisy port on the Dallas PE"

ontology / grounding:
  PhysicalPort: PE-DAL-01/xe-0/0/3

declared action:
  SHUT_PORT

Execution Control:
  authorize SHUT_PORT on this PhysicalPort

Action binding:
  Juniper -> gNMI/NETCONF implementation
  Ericsson -> vendor-specific implementation

Evidence:
  declared_action = SHUT_PORT
  intended state  = admin_down
  observed state  = ifAdminStatus=down
```

This is the architecture's answer to “LLM tool calling”: the agent may select a **declared semantic operation**; only the platform can turn that operation into a registered callable against an owning SoR.

