## RAN / spectrum example — traffic steering between cells and carriers

“Switch towers” is useful shorthand, but in a mobile network the more precise operation is usually **network-controlled handover / traffic steering between serving and neighboring cells**, potentially across different frequency carriers. A UE may move from a source cell to a target cell based on radio measurements, load, interference, service requirements, and network policy. At a broader level, load-balancing logic can alter mobility parameters so traffic shifts away from a congested or impaired cell toward neighbors with capacity.

Scenario: a sector on `SITE-DAL-17` experiences persistent interference and congestion on its mid-band NR carrier. Neighboring cells have spare capacity. The platform should reduce customer impact without allowing an LLM to issue arbitrary RAN commands.

