## Rules

0. A pod implements its conformance envelope, not all six layers; it must still conform to the authority contracts it consumes.

1. Cognition never mutates.
2. No resolved instance → no lock → no write.
3. Every mutation has an idempotency key and a risk class.
4. Owner of the object beats a better plan from another agent.
5. Human takeover is lock plus session transfer, not a chat interrupt.
6. Success is intended versus actual, not a southbound status code.
7. One ontology. No second world-model in the prompt.
8. SoRs own writes. The SoT is a projection. The model is neither.

Refuse: a single box that plans and commits; MCP as a substitute for Execution; LangGraph state as inventory; embeddings as grounding; one chat API for NOC, CSR, and machines; an Action layer that is only a prompt-selected HTTP client; a Knowledge graph that originates customers, circuits, or change records.

---

## Build order

Do not start by making Cognition smarter.

1. Ontology slice and inventory graph for one domain.
2. SoT projection over the real SoRs, with provenance and freshness — not a new database of record.
3. Action adapters with dry-run and diffs for a few high-value verbs.
4. Execution Control: keys, authority, ledger.
5. Ownership and locks.
6. Grounding on the Cognition–Knowledge edge.
7. Cognition last — LangGraph and the model on a kernel that can already say no.

A mediocre planner behind a hard execution gate is safer than a brilliant planner with edit-config in its pocket.

Keep the original layering. Complete it. North and south stay. The middle splits into think, own, authorize, and act. Ontology constrains every layer and lives in one place. Grounding is the only honest way language meets inventory. LangGraph and the model stay in Cognition. Systems of Record stay southbound. The Source of Truth is the projection you plan against, not a second OSS.

The interesting question is no longer which model is in the box. It is whether the box is allowed to touch the network without going through a kernel that can refuse.


## How the L1–L6 layers prevent drift

Drift is not one failure mode. In an agentic carrier platform it can appear as semantic drift, ownership drift, workflow drift, execution drift, truth drift, or evaluation drift.

All six layers participate, but they play different roles:

| Layer | Primary drift it prevents | Mechanism |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **L1 — Experience & external contracts** | **Contract / interaction drift** | Stable northbound schemas, UI/API contracts, explicit response surfaces, and versioned external behavior prevent agents or pods from silently changing what callers can ask for or what the platform promises back. |
| **L2 — Agent & cognition** | **Semantic / model drift** | Closed operation enums, typed slots, structured outputs, ontology-shaped interpretation, and bounded cognition prevent a model/provider/prompt change from silently redefining executable meaning. |
| **L3 — Control & governance** | **Ownership / lifecycle / workflow-authority drift** | Canonical task identity, continuum law, delivery mode, grounding, reservations, handoff completeness, claim semantics, policy, and admissibility prevent local agents from inventing their own ownership or transition rules. |
| **L4 — Action & integration** | **Execution / adapter drift** | Declared-action contracts, typed schemas, registered callable bindings, recovery semantics, protocol adapters, and SoR ownership prevent vendor/API changes from leaking upward into semantic behavior. |
| **L5 — Systems, knowledge & operational truth** | **Identity / ontology / state drift** | Canonical ontology types, grounded live instances, provenance, freshness/TTL, SoT projections, and SoR boundaries prevent reasoning over stale, ambiguous, or shadow state. |
| **L6 — Assurance & evaluation** | **Behavioral / regression / policy drift over time** | Hard invariants, behavioral reliability tests, cognition goldens, trajectory tests, adversarial simulation, production evidence, and failure-to-invariant ratchets detect drift and progressively turn recurring failures into stronger deterministic controls. |

The architectural distinction is:

> **L1–L5 are drift-containment surfaces. L6 is the drift-detection and ratcheting surface.**

Another way to view it:

```text
MODEL / PROMPT CHANGES
        ↓
L2 constrains semantic drift

WORKFLOW / AGENT CHANGES
        ↓
L3 constrains ownership and continuum drift

VENDOR / API CHANGES
        ↓
L4 contains execution drift behind stable declared actions

DATA / NETWORK CHANGES
        ↓
L5 exposes freshness, provenance, and current truth

EXTERNAL PRODUCT CHANGES
        ↓
L1 protects northbound contracts

ANY OF THE ABOVE REGRESS OVER TIME
        ↓
L6 detects, measures, and promotes failures into stronger invariants
```

This means **drift prevention is not owned by one governance layer.** The reference architecture deliberately places a stable contract at every seam where one kind of authority changes into another.

A useful conformance question for every pod is:

> **What can drift at this layer, what contract contains it, and what L6 evidence proves the contract still holds?**
